# LLP 0013 Hermes patch 0005 — native-compartment refinements
# Class: B (a POD flag + accessors on Runtime; a new native function) + C (the
#         GetGlobalObject fast-path guard). @ref LLP 0013#mechanism-2 — (Phase 3)
#
# Refinements on the native compartments landed in patch 0004:
#
#   * Hot-path guard. Runtime gains `anyCompartmentActive_` (set the first time a
#     Domain is given a compartment). While false, the interpreter's
#     globalForFrame returns the real global directly, so code that never uses
#     compartments (the common case) pays a single predicted-not-taken branch
#     instead of the Domain walk. __exactSetCompartmentFor arms the flag. Once
#     armed, a per-Domain POD `hasCompartmentGlobal()` bit (patch 0004) is the
#     second-tier guard: frames whose own Domain has no compartment still skip the
#     GCPointer read barrier on `compartmentGlobal_`, so root/first-party code does
#     not pay the full Domain walk just because a dependency installed a
#     compartment. Without it, the one-way `anyCompartmentActive_` flag makes every
#     global access take the slow Domain-walk path for the whole process once any
#     compartment exists (ENG-22948).
#   * Native freeze primitive. `__exactNativeFreeze(obj)` (JSObject::freeze), the
#     building block for a faster / boot-time lockdown; the userland freeze walk
#     can call it per intrinsic.
#   * getCurrentCompartmentGlobal(Runtime&): the compartment of the nearest user
#     frame's Domain — the helper a future eval/Function call-site binding will
#     use. (Binding produced code is intentionally NOT done in runBytecode: the
#     eval'd code's caller frame is not walkable there, so it documents that the
#     capture must happen at the eval call site; eval/Function are contained by
#     withholding + lockdown taming in the meantime.)
